course: dictionary
lesson: 12915
title: Respected and admired: the word esteemed
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words sound grand and polite, and you hear them at special events. Esteemed is one of them. Read the words around it: «Our esteemed guests arrived at the school hall.» The guests are being treated with special respect, so esteemed must mean greatly respected and admired.
explain: Esteemed can also be a verb form. The word esteem means to respect someone highly, so esteemed is what happened in the past. «The village esteemed Nurse Amy for her kindness.» The village respected Amy a lot, because of her kindness. Try the swap: «The village respected Amy for her kindness» still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
explain: You will often hear esteemed in speeches, letters and ceremonies, and before job titles such as «esteemed colleague». Sometimes people use it as a joke, to sound extra grand. The context tells you which one it is.
fact: Esteemed means greatly respected and admired.
fact: Esteemed is an adjective when it describes a person: «an esteemed scientist».
fact: Esteemed is also the past form of esteem, which means to respect someone highly.
fact: Esteemed is a formal word, often used in speeches, letters and ceremonies.
fact: Phrases to know: «highly esteemed», «esteemed colleague», «esteemed guest».
exercise: «The esteemed scientist gave a talk about the stars.» What does esteemed mean here? | *Greatly respected and admired | Sold cheaply at a shop | Lost in a maze | Very tired after a long day
exercise: Where would you most likely hear the word esteemed? | At a muddy puddle race | *At a speech in a grand hall | At a bedtime story | At the kick-off of a football match
exercise: «The town esteemed her for her kindness.» Which word tells you esteemed is the past form of esteem? | *esteemed | town | her | kindness
test: «Her esteemed colleague gave the opening speech.» Which phrase is closest in meaning to esteemed colleague? | a quite noisy colleague | *a greatly respected colleague | a rather lazy colleague | a very cold colleague
test: «The team esteemed Mr Patel for his hard work.» What does esteemed mean here? | Teased | *Respected highly | Missed | Pushed
exam: Sometimes people use esteemed as a joke, as in «Our esteemed dog has eaten the cake.» Why is the word funny here? | It is a question about the dog | *It makes a silly thing sound grand and important | It means the cake was huge | It means the dog is very sad
